WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:06.399
A phobia is an excessive fear of a seemingly
harmless thing and those affected, go to great

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:09.050
lengths to avoid certain situations.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:19.110
If a situation cannot be avoided, they experience
severe distress, panic attacks or even fainting.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:24.510
Phobias are less prevalent among children
and older people, and more common among teenagers,

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.500
and especially girls.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:34.860
They run in families or can be the result
of a traumatic experience early in life.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.180
Phobias can be categorized into 3 types.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.720
A social phobia is when we fear others, especially
their judgment.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:52.510
Agoraphobia is the fear of a situation we
perceive to be unsafe or difficult to escape.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:58.180
Specific phobias include fears of certain
things, or situations, such as heights, or

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.739
injuries and seeing blood.

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:11.920
Some phobias are especially complicated — such
as the famous case of the Button Boy.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.079
It was a normal day at kindergarten when a
6 year old boy ran out of buttons to paste

00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:23.340
on his poster board and was asked to come
to the front of the class to get some more.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.710
When he reached for the bowl, his hand slipped
and all the buttons fell on him.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:35.990
For the boy this was a deeply traumatic event.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.450
Not long after, he didn’t want to dress
himself anymore.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:45.579
He began to have difficulty concentrating
in class due to an excessive preoccupation

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:49.579
with not touching his school uniform.

00:01:49.579 --> 00:01:56.060
And eventually, he started avoiding people
just to stay away from their buttons!

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:01.310
Life became so difficult that 4 years after
the incident the boy and his mother decided

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.729
to seek professional help.

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:10.030
The psychiatrists Lissette Saavedra and Wendy
Silverman who studied the case were able to

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:16.341
rule out obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD),
which is often confused with a phobia, and

00:02:16.341 --> 00:02:22.440
concluded that the boy must have developed
a speciﬁc phobia for buttons.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:29.200
To treat the boy, the doctors invited the
mother and son to be part of a research project.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.430
To assess the child’s progress throughout
the treatment, they used a Feelings Thermometer,

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:40.920
allowing the boy to grade each interaction
from 1, feeling happy, to 9, feeling angry.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:47.750
Big, brass jeans buttons hardly bothered the
boy, but small, clear, plastic ones horrified

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:48.830
him.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:55.220
After the boy’s feelings were better understood,
the doctors selected a treatment method.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.350
First they tried behavioral exposure therapy,
a form of classical conditioning

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:04.980
Whenever the boy touched a button successfully,
he was rewarded.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.340
They then repeated the process in various
forms.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:15.360
After 4 sessions, the boy completed all tasks
and he was able to interact with way more

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.290
buttons than before. However, he felt more
distressed about buttons than ever.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:27.280
When the doctors talked to the boy again he
revealed that he found buttons disgusting

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.340
and that they emitted a bad smell.
And so they wondered: did the exposure therapy

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.620
reduce his fear, but increase his feeling
of disgust?

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:43.780
Next, they tried Imagery Exposure Therapy.
For the seven sessions that followed, the

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:49.490
boy was prompted to just visualize buttons
falling on him and imagine how they looked,

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.340
felt, and smelled.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:58.850
This time the boy’s self-reported levels
of distress reduced dramatically.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:04.290
Even 12-months after the therapy the boy reported
minimal fear of buttons.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:09.980
So why did the first treatment fail, while
the second one worked?

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:16.359
The first treatment changed the minds' expectations
of what harmless buttons may trigger —classical

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:21.900
conditioning. The second therapy changed the
mind’s evaluation of buttons themselves

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.439
– that they aren’t actually that disgusting.

00:04:25.439 --> 00:04:31.960
In technical terms, expectancy-learning failed,
but evaluative learning, succeeded.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:38.520
But treatment of a phobia may take many forms.
Social- and agoraphobia are often treated

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:42.419
with counseling or even medication.

00:04:42.419 --> 00:04:48.520
Specific phobias are often treated by systematic
desensitization, today often with the help

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.800
of virtual reality.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.580
Humor is another serious therapy that often
works.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:01.909
Activities that elicit humor with the feared
situation, can help reduce disgust, anxiety

00:05:01.909 --> 00:05:06.699
or whatever other uncovered feelings may be
the cause of our phobia.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:13.340
What’s your experience with phobia? Have
you ever had one that you were able to overcome?

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:33.909
And if so, how did you do it? Share your thoughts
in the comments below.

